Standards of Professionalism
The Court appreciates a civil and professional discourse on all matters. Court hearings begin promptly at the appointed time. All parties should assume the Court has read the subject matter pleading and attached case law and is ready to hear argument. All attorneys who appear before the court shall adhere to the Twelfth Judicial Circuit’s
Standards of Professionalism
, also available on the websites of the
Manatee County Bar Association
and the
Sarasota County Bar Association
. The court requires attorneys to strictly adhere to Chapter 4 of the Rules Regulating the Florida Bar. The Court and the judicial assistant’s goal is to manage the cases assigned to Division 5 in a professional, efficient and prompt manner.
A. Remote Appearance
Zoom Appearances
All matters are held in person in courtroom 4A in the Manatee County Judicial Center. Zoom appearances are only permitted pursuant to the Administrative Rules of Court, and upon order of the Court; zoom appearances are generally limited to expert witness testimony upon agreement of the parties. See the
Technology Services page
to request an IT Zoom presentation cart.
B. Hearing Procedures
All motions shall be filed prior to reserving hearing time.
Caselaw shall be submitted to the court at least 3 days prior to the hearing date via email at
ANelson@jud12.flcourts.org
. Please CC opposing counsel in your email submission.
If an attorney fails to appear at a scheduled hearing on the time scheduled, the Court will Deny the Motion without prejudice.
Scheduling
Hearings are strictly limited to the time reserved.
Please note that the party opposing the motion is entitled to equal time; therefore, the party reserving and scheduling the hearing should reserve the hearing time accordingly. In the alternative, the scheduling party should confer with opposing counsel and agree regarding the total time required.
Cross Noticed Hearings and Substitute Motions are Permitted in Limited Circumstances
Once a motion is scheduled via JACS or the Judicial Assistant, no subsequent motions are permitted to be “cross noticed,” absent prior approval of the Court and opposing counsel. Any cross noticed hearing notice shall acknowledge these requirements.
Once a particular motion is scheduled via JACS or the Judicial Assistant, another motion shall not be “substituted” in the event the original motion is cancelled absent prior approval by the Court or opposing counsel.
Add-On Cases/Hearings
The Court and its judicial Assistant strive to be readily accessible. Accordingly. Any party wishing to add a case or hearing to the docket after JACS has closed must contact the JA no later than 11 am the day before the scheduled hearings. Cases may not be added on without the express consent of opposing counsel and the Court. For example, to place an additional case on the Tuesday docket the requesting party shall contact the Judicial Assistant no later than 11 am on Monday. The requesting party must have the approval of opposing counsel prior to contacting the Judicial Assistant.
Do not contact the Clerk's office to add-on cases. If approved, the Court will contact the Clerk.
Hearing Cancellation Policy
Please cancel previously scheduling hearing time through JACS as soon as possible. If unable to cancel the hearing through JACS, please contact the Judicial Assistant at
ANelson@jud12.flcourts.org
so she can make the time available for other cases.
The attorney cancelling the hearing is responsible for notifying the Sheriff’s Deputies in judicial security that an inmate will not be needed. Please provide sufficient notice of a cancelled hearing so that the defendant is not transported.
Procedure for Setting Hearings
All pleas and hearings, thirty (30) minutes or less shall be set by using JACS. It is recommended that 10-minute slots be used for negotiated pleas, Motions to Compel and any other simple motions that will take 10 minutes or less to be heard.
Hearings of sixty (60) minutes or more must be scheduled through the Judicial Assistant.
All paperwork from the ASA (Assistant State Attorney), PD (Public Defender), or DOC (Department of Corrections) office must be sent via regular rounds except in emergency situations.
Pleas and Motions
All pleas and motions shall be set on those days designated for pleas and motions.
Pleas and motions dates are assigned to a specific Assistant State Attorney. The scheduling party must determine which Assistant State Attorney is assigned to a case and schedule his/her pleas or motion accordingly. Please consult the online calendar before setting a case for hearing.
Any hearing set on a day not assigned to the specific Assistant State Attorney will be cancelled.
The Court is aware it may be necessary to set pleas and motions for dates other than those designated to a specific Assistant State Attorney. However, on such rare occasions, the scheduling party must:
Contact the Judicial Assistant with: the case number; the name of the assigned Assistant State Attorney; the reason it cannot be placed on a designated date; and, the time needed. The Judicial Assistant will then provide several potential alternate dates and times,
Contact the assigned Assistant State Attorney to clear the date and time,
Re-contact the Judicial Assistant to confirm the date and time utilized. (NOTE: The Judicial Assistant will only hold the potential dates and times for twenty-four (24) hours. If no confirmation is made the potential dates and times will be re-opened).
Plea Cut-off Date
: The plea cut-off date for all cases set on a trial docket will be 5 pm the Friday before trial week.
Bond Hearings
Jail Bond Hearings
: The Court will now (
effective immediately
) conduct Jail bond hearings requiring 5 minutes or less at Case Management in addition to weekly on Thursdays at 8:30 or 9 a.m. Please review the
calendar
for details. Any other type of hearings set in this time slot will be cancelled. However, Motions to Release Uncharged Defendants may be scheduled during this time.
Bond Hearings in Excess of Ten (10) Minutes
: Bond hearings requiring time more than ten (10) minutes shall be scheduled in accordance with the procedures established under the
Scheduling
section.
Motions for Rehearing, Reconsideration, Arrest of Judgment and/or New Trial
All Motions for Rehearing, Reconsideration, Arrest of Judgment and/or New Trial must cite the applicable Florida Rule of Criminal Procedure or other grounds upon which relief is sought and shall be submitted directly to the judicial assistant, along with a cover letter. After review, the judge will determine if a hearing is required.
Motions/Orders for Release of Uncharged Defendants
Motions for Release of Uncharged Defendants shall be filed with the clerk and a clerk’s date/time stamp copy delivered to the judge’s chambers and to the ASA. Defense counsel shall obtain hearing time from the judicial assistant for the next scheduled video bond hearing or at any motion time cleared with the ASA. Should the ASA file charges before the hearing, the ASA shall deliver a clerk’s date/time stamped copy of the Information to the Defense and the judge’s chambers, in order to cancel the hearing.
These hearings may be set during the Video Bond time.
ro Se
Motions to Modify or Terminate Probation
All unrepresented (Pro-se) Defendants who file a Motion to Modify or Terminate Probation should expect the following: Upon receipt of a pro se Motion to Modify or Terminate Probation, the Court will take the following action(s):
Assign a Motion Hearing Date
: Scheduled at least 4 weeks from date of motion. These hearings will be held on a scheduled VOP Conference date.
Send a Notice of Hearing to the Defendant
: A copy of the Motion and the Notice of Hearing will be sent to both the State Attorney’s office and the Department of Corrections for review. The SAO is responsible for notifying any victims. The Court requires attendance from the State Attorney’s Office and DOC at the hearing.
C. Communications with the Court
Submission of Orders and Stipulations to Continue
Counsel is encouraged to use Stipulations to Continue the Case Management Conference, particularly for in-custody Defendants. This office utilizes a division email for standard Stipulations to Continue Case Management (received a minimum of three days prior to the scheduled date), fully executed Stipulations for Substitution of Counsel, and extended hearing time requests.
If the parties stipulate to a continuance in Court with a Defendant who is in-custody and present for court, the Defendant’s attorney shall immediately notify the courtroom deputies of the continuance.
Following a hearing, counsel may submit proposed orders via email only if instructed to do so. Submission of orders and other documents shall be done by regular mail, overnight services, or hand-delivery absent an emergency or unless given permission by Judge Flowers. This office does not provide electronic copies of Orders. Attorneys who fail to provide conforming copies and envelopes must retrieve their orders via Attorney/Subscriber Access (Manatee County) or ClerkNet (Sarasota.) The offices of the Public Defender and the State Attorney should deliver and retrieve documents via Daily Rounds on the 9th floor.
D. Submission of Orders
Electronic Portal (E-Portal) Submission of Proposed Orders
Pursuant to Administrative Order 2022-1.2, judges in the Twelfth Circuit must accept submission of proposed Orders through the Florida e-Portal. The Court will process all proposed Orders (Paper and electronically filed) in a timely fashion and all orders must be reviewed and processed by the judicial assistant before providing them to the Judge.
All ePortal submissions must include a cover letter and are limited to uncontested/stipulated Orders. All other orders will be rejected by the judicial assistant.
E. Courtesy Copies
Counsel must provide via regular mail, overnight services, or hand-delivery courtesy paper copies of all hearing documents (notices of hearing, motions, case law, and other supporting documents) at least three days prior to the hearing to allow sufficient time for judicial review.
F. Emergency & Other Urgent Matters
The judicial assistant is not authorized to offer or schedule emergency or expedited hearing time.
The moving attorney is required to confer with opposing counsel in a good faith effort to resolve the issue prior to filing an emergency motion. Any emergency motion must include a certificate of good faith that states the movant has contacted opposing counsel and his/her position on the relief sought. Requests for emergency or expedited hearing time must be made by contacting the JA by phone or email and providing a copy of the contested motion. The Judge will review the motion to determine whether an expedited or emergency hearing is warranted.
G. Exhibits for Evidentiary Proceedings
In cases where there are numerous documents to be entered into evidence as exhibits, the Court requires the parties to confer with the Clerk of Court prior to trial and present the Clerk with an exhibit list as well as the exhibits. Additionally, the Court requires the parties to provide courtesy paper copies of the exhibit list and marked exhibits to opposing counsel, as well as the Court.
If either side is going to use an exhibit that is in a different form/format than has been prepared and submitted with discovery the Court requires an exact copy of the exhibit to be delivered to opposing counsel no less than five (5) business days before trial. Counsel shall disclose to opposing counsel where in the discovery the information contained in the newly formatted exhibit can be found.
